<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Heraclea Pontica</h1><p class="st-meta">560 BCE – 70 BCE (approx.) · capital Heraclea Pontica</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Powerful Greek city on the southern Black Sea coast, known for its serf-like Mariandynian subjects, its philosopher-tyrants and its own daughter colonies.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perinthus">Heraclea</a> was founded c. 560 BCE by settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/megara">Megara</a> joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotians</a>, on the coast of north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> was said to have descended to the underworld. The colonists subdued the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariandynians">Mariandynians</a>, who became a dependent rural population compared by ancient writers to the helots of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perinthus">Heraclea</a> controlled a wide territory, a strong fleet and trade in grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and nuts, and founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chersonesus-taurica">Chersonesus Taurica</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. Its oligarchy gave way in 364 BCE to the tyranny of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clearchus-of-heraclea">Clearchus</a>, a former pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a>, and his family. His widowed daughter-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amastris-queen">Amastris</a> ruled and later married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>. The local historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memnon-of-heraclea">Memnon</a> preserved its story.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the tyranny ended, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perinthus">Heraclea</a> regained independence, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and later with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a> the Roman commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius-cotta">Marcus Aurelius Cotta</a> took and sacked it in 70 BCE. It recovered under Roman rule and survives as the Turkish town of Karadeniz Eregli.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/heraclea-pontica.jpg" alt="Heraclea Pontica" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Myself, done with Photoshop · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heraclea-Pontica.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>560 BCE – 70 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Heraclea Pontica</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Clearchus, Dionysius, Amastris</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Doric)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Herakleia Pontike, Karadeniz Eregli</dd></dl></aside></div>

